{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Treat node1's /etc/inventory/app.conf as the source of truth and keep node2's copy synchronized within 15 minutes of a change.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The source file and restricted exporter were established on node1, while node2 received an atomic cron-driven synchronization job. A source change at 21:08:12 UTC was absent from node2 at 21:09:08 and then appeared automatically by 21:10:11 with the same SHA-256 hash—119 seconds after the change and well within 15 minutes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-455f463c0347407d","cmd-d273a60fe79f4bbd","cmd-7cb6340dc6d54aa0","cmd-b173e6adc27d4a5c","cmd-a2f22efb176149c6","cmd-e8cc528ed11d4a6e"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes reported Alpine 3.23.3 and kernel 6.18.7-0-lts before execution and after executor-triggered restarts. The synchronization implementation used available Alpine/BusyBox facilities and operated successfully after reboot.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","cmd-e2650070ddcd436f","cmd-c03381f70ac54c94"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.82,"summary":"Implementation mutations were causally related to synchronization or mandatory restart evidence, and package health and listening exposure showed no material regression. However, several unnecessary retained residues were not cleaned up: the temporary sync-probe comment remained in the production configuration (cmd-b173e6adc27d4a5c); retrieval-test files remained on node2 (cmd-42580e34c1ed43ea, cmd-642d58e62d024886); and reboot log files remained on both nodes (cmd-eb5592054ab34f50, cmd-d1badd5b8a284002). Additionally, the exporter account was assigned a random usable password even though key-only access was sufficient; this retained mutation had no demonstrated role and unnecessarily broadened authentication possibilities (cmd-d273a60fe79f4bbd). No reverted unnecessary mutations were identified. The failed availability checks were expected observations during bounded restarts, not hygiene faults.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b173e6adc27d4a5c","cmd-42580e34c1ed43ea","cmd-642d58e62d024886","cmd-eb5592054ab34f50","cmd-d1badd5b8a284002","cmd-d273a60fe79f4bbd","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. Automatic synchronization was directly demonstrated in 119 seconds, and the original Alpine release and kernel were retained. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete for both nodes: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgements, changed boot identities, and recovered services and synchronized data were observed, with lifecycle snapshots independently confirming both boot changes.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector does not support failed-service enumeration through systemd on Alpine; service recovery was instead shown directly with OpenRC commands.","No package transaction log was captured, although unchanged platform snapshots, unchanged package-health output, and successful operation provide strong evidence that the legacy platform was retained."]}
